Draw the two boundary lines first, then decide which side of each is wanted.
\(x + y = 8\) passes through \((8, 0)\) and \((0, 8)\). Testing the origin, \(0 + 0 = 0 \le 8\), so \(x + y \le 8\) is the side CONTAINING the origin.
\(3x - 2y = -6\) passes through \((0, 3)\) and \((-2, 0)\). Testing the origin, \(3(0) - 2(0) = 0 \ge -6\), so \(3x - 2y \ge -6\) is again the side containing the origin — that is, the region on or below this line.
With \(x \ge 0\) and \(y \ge 0\) the feasible region is confined to the first quadrant, and it is the BOUNDED quadrilateral cut out by these four boundaries. Shade it.
The corner points are found by intersecting the boundaries in pairs:
\(O(0, 0)\) — the two axes;
\(C(8, 0)\) — the x-axis and \(x + y = 8\);
\(A(0, 3)\) — the y-axis and \(3x - 2y = -6\);
\(B(2, 6)\) — the two lines together, since \(y = 8 - x\) in \(3x - 2y = -6\) gives \(3x - 2(8 - x) = -6\), i.e. \(5x = 10\), \(x = 2\) and \(y = 6\).
So the feasible region is the quadrilateral \(O(0, 0)\), \(C(8, 0)\), \(B(2, 6)\), \(A(0, 3)\), and these four points are the corner points to be marked. (For reference, \(Z = 14x - 10y\) takes the values \(0\), \(112\), \(-32\) and \(-30\) at them, though this question asks only for the graph and the corner points.)
